Summary

Your API has been completely restructured from a monolithic route handler to a production-ready layered architecture following enterprise best practices.

Files Created (10)

Shared Infrastructure:
- src/errors/app-error.ts — Typed error class (status, code, message, details)
- src/middleware/async-handler.ts — Wrapper for async handlers (prevents hanging requests)
- src/middleware/validate.ts — Zod validation middleware (applies defaults, strict mode)
- src/middleware/error-handler.ts — Central error handler (catches all errors consistently)

Orders Resource (Layered):
- src/schemas/orders.schema.ts — Zod schemas (single source of truth)
- src/repositories/orders.repository.ts — Repository interface + in-memory Map implementation
- src/services/orders.service.ts — Business logic (no Express types, no storage access)
- src/routes/orders.routes.ts — HTTP handlers (validates, calls service, maps responses)
- src/routes/orders.routes.test.ts — 16 integration tests (success, validation, 404, edge cases)

Documentation:
- README.md — Comprehensive production-ready guide

Files Modified (3)

- src/app.ts — Added Helmet, request body limits, error middleware mounting
- CLAUDE.md — Updated architecture section with layered structure
- package.json — Added Helmet for security headers (production-grade)

Verification Results

✓ TypeScript strict mode:  All files compile
✓ Tests:                   16/16 passed
✓ Security audit:          0 vulnerabilities

Architecture Highlights

Layering (no shortcuts):
Routes → Services → Repositories
(HTTP)  (Logic)     (Storage)

Type Safety:
- Strict TypeScript mode (no any)
- Zod for runtime validation
- .strict() on schemas to prevent mass assignment

Error Handling:
- Standard error response format with codes
- Centralized error handler middleware
- No stack traces or internal details in responses

Security:
- Input validation at all boundaries (body, params)
- Body size limit (10KB)
- Security headers via Helmet
- Request validation with .strict() to reject unknown fields

Testing:
- Integration tests (real HTTP layer)
- Independent tests (reset store in beforeEach)
- Comprehensive coverage (happy path + errors)

What's Missing (By Design)

These production features are in the target standard but skipped per instructions:

- Rate limiting: express-rate-limit (add when needed)
- Logging: Structured JSON logging with pino (ready to integrate)
- Auth: JWT/OAuth2 bearer tokens + per-route checks
- Database: Swap in-memory Map for a DB by implementing IOrderRepository
- Graceful shutdown: SIGTERM/SIGINT handlers in server.ts

The architecture supports all of these—they drop in without touching routes, services, or schemas.

Next Steps

1. Add a database: Implement IOrderRepository for PostgreSQL/MongoDB (services stay unchanged)
2. Add logging: Integrate Pino logger (error middleware ready to log details)
3. Add authentication: Create auth middleware, check ownership in services
4. Deploy: Use npm ci + npm run test:run + npm audit in your CI/CD pipeline

